-- title:
--   Time-zero span $\mathcal E_0=\mathrm{span}\{e^{i\Phi(f_0)}\}$
-- statement:
--   The **time-zero span** is
--   $$\mathcal E_0=\mathrm{span}\{\exp(i\Phi(f_0)):\ f\in C_c^\infty(\Sigma,\mathbb R)\},$$
--   the (not closed) span of the exponentials of the time-zero sharp distributions. Theorem 4.4 says that quantization maps it isometrically onto a dense subspace of $\mathcal H$.
-- source:
--   Jaffe, Ritter, Quantum Field Theory on Curved Backgrounds. I. The Euclidean Functional Integral, arXiv:hep-th/0609003v1, Theorem 4.4, p. 24; proof, p. 25

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_expVec
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_sharpTime
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_IsSliceTestFunction

open scoped Manifold ContDiff ENNReal Topology
open Bundle MeasureTheory

namespace JRCurvedQFT.SharpTime

variable {d : ℕ} {M : Type} [EMetricSpace M]
  [ChartedSpace (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) M] [IsManifold 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) ∞ M]
  [RiemannianBundle (fun (x : M) ↦ TangentSpace 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) x)]

/-- The **time-zero span** `ℰ₀ = span{exp(iΦ(f₀)) : f ∈ C_c^∞(Σ, ℝ)}` (not closed).

Jaffe, Ritter, *Quantum Field Theory on Curved Backgrounds. I. The Euclidean Functional Integral*, arXiv:hep-th/0609003v1, Theorem 4.4, p. 24: "the vectors exp(iΦ(f₀)) ... quantization maps the span of these
vectors isometrically onto ℋ"; p. 25: `ℰ_t`, "the subspace generated by vectors of the form
exp(iΦ(f_t))".

**Formalization Note** Reading 2 (`f` real, `C_c^∞(Σ)` via ξ-invariant lifts). -/
noncomputable def timeZeroSpan [MeasurableSpace M] (S : StaticStructure (d := d) M)
    (μ : Measure M) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsFiniteMeasure P]
    (Φ : ((M → ℝ) → ℝ) → Ω → ℝ) : Submodule ℂ (Lp ℂ 2 P) :=
  Submodule.span ℂ {A | ∃ f : M → ℝ, IsSliceTestFunction S f ∧
    A = expVec P Φ (sharpTime S μ f 0)}

end JRCurvedQFT.SharpTime


